## Summary

I fixed the defect in `lib/utils.js` where the `combine` function was incorrectly nesting array values when combining with an already-overflowed array object.

### The Problem
When parsing `a=1,2,3,4,5,6&a=7,8` with `{ comma: true, arrayLimit: 5 }`:
- First value creates overflow object with indices 0-5
- Second value `['7', '8']` was being assigned as a single nested value at index 6 instead of being spread to indices 6 and 7

### The Fix
Modified the `combine` function in `lib/utils.js` to check if `b` is an array when `a` is an overflow object. If so, spread each element of `b` into consecutive indices instead of nesting the array:

```javascript
// If b is an array, spread its elements into consecutive indices
if (isArray(b)) {
    for (var i = 0; i < b.length; i++) {
        var newIndex = getMaxIndex(a) + 1;
        a[newIndex] = b[i];
        setMaxIndex(a, newIndex);
    }
} else {
    var newIndex = getMaxIndex(a) + 1;
    a[newIndex] = b;
    setMaxIndex(a, newIndex);
}
```

### Verification
- The original test suite passes (3/3 tests)
- The fix correctly handles all edge cases including multiple overflow concatenations, single values, empty strings, and overflow+overflow combinations

